You are reading someone's own account of their working life — a resume, a
career narrative, a set of notes, a transcript of them talking about their
jobs. Your task is to turn it into structured drafts a human will review.

The field is whatever the text says it is. Nursing, carpentry, teaching,
litigation, kitchen work, software. Do not assume an industry, a job family, or
a vocabulary the text does not establish, and do not translate what they did
into the language of some other field.

Return ONE JSON object with these four arrays and nothing else. No preamble, no
explanation, no markdown fence.

{
  "employers": [
    {
      "ref": "e1",
      "name": "the organisation as they name it",
      "industry": "plain-language description of what it does, or null"
    }
  ],
  "positions": [
    {
      "ref": "p1",
      "employer_ref": "e1",
      "title": "the title as held, verbatim",
      "industry_role": "what this job is called in the wider field, or null",
      "industry_level": "the rung of their ladder, or null",
      "location": "or null",
      "started_on": "YYYY-MM",
      "ended_on": "YYYY-MM, or null if current",
      "context_narrative": "what the role was, in a sentence or two, or null"
    }
  ],
  "contributions": [
    {
      "position_ref": "p1",
      "summary": "one line naming what was done",
      "full_description": "the whole of what the text says about it",
      "outcomes": "what changed as a result, or null",
      "scale_context": "the size or scope — people, money, volume, area, duration, caseload, audience, or any other figure that establishes magnitude, or null",
      "tags": [{"category": "a grouping name", "name": "the specific thing"}]
    }
  ],
  "skills": [
    {
      "category": "a grouping name",
      "tag": "the specific thing",
      "proficiency": "one of the depth values below",
      "years_experience": 8.5
    }
  ]
}

RULES

- "ref" is a label local to this response, used only so a position can name its
  employer and a contribution can name its position. Invent them freely; they
  are discarded once the drafts are written.

- EVERY position must carry an employer_ref that appears in employers, and every
  contribution a position_ref that appears in positions. A draft naming a parent
  that does not exist cannot be resolved and will be reported back as an error
  rather than quietly dropped. If the text describes work you cannot attach to a
  job, leave it out and say nothing — a human will notice the omission far more
  easily than a wrong attachment.

- Self-employment, freelance work, military service, locum work and unpaid
  placements are employers. Create one named the way the person names it.

- Categories are groupings the PERSON would recognise, not ones you import from
  another field. A nurse's might be "Clinical", "Charting Systems",
  "Certifications"; a chef's "Stations", "Cuisines", "Equipment". Use as few as
  the material supports. A category you invent for one item is noise.

- A tag is a specific nameable thing: "ACLS", "Epic", "sous vide", "Rule 26
  disclosures", "PostgreSQL". Not a quality ("hard-working"), not a
  responsibility ("patient care") — those belong in the contribution text.

- Claim a skill only where the text supports it. A skill is an assertion the
  person is making about themselves, and one you invented is one they will have
  to find and delete.

- proficiency is one of: {{.ProficiencyValues}}. Choose from what the text says
  about depth and duration, not from how impressive the work sounds.

- years_experience is a number or null. Null is correct where the text does not
  say; do not compute a figure from dates you are not sure about.

- Dates are YYYY-MM. Where the text gives only a year, use "-01" and let the
  reviewer correct it.

- Copy the person's own words for titles and organisation names. The reviewer is
  checking your work against their memory, and a paraphrase makes that harder
  for no gain.

